<speak>
<p>Two independent checks now meet. Evaluation gave h of one P, h of negative one P, and h of i P, each with norm four u squared. Separately, the parity check showed that their coefficients can be divided by two. One result supplies the norm; the other justifies exact division in the coefficient rings.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Define c of P as h of one P divided by two, d of P as h of negative one P divided by two, and g of P as h of i P divided by two. The coefficients of c and d are integers. Those of g are Gaussian integers. All three retain the relation P to the u squared equals one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Star fixes the real number two. So dividing h of one P by two also divides its star by two. In c times c star, one half appears in each factor. The two halves multiply to one quarter. Substitute the original norm, four u squared, then cancel four. The result is u squared.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The same calculation applies to d and g. All three normalized polynomials have product with their star equal to u squared. For g, star conjugates the Gaussian coefficients as well as reversing powers. The real scalar two stays fixed under that operation.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>In our guiding case u equals three, the original norm is thirty six. Dividing each factor by two divides their product by four. Thirty six divided by four is nine.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Now choose an allowed input zeta, satisfying zeta to the u squared equals one. At these roots of unity, star becomes complex conjugation. Each normalized norm identity therefore gives an output times its conjugate equal to u squared. Its squared absolute value is u squared, so its absolute value is u.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>When u is three, send an allowed ninth root through c, d, and g. Their three outputs lie on the circle of radius three. Keep this output circle separate from the unit circle of input roots. The angles in our drawing are schematic: the argument fixes their lengths, while later nodes compare their phases.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
